      "text": "To find \\(\\mathbf{A}^{100}\\), we first need to understand the behavior of the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) when it is raised to successive powers. Let's start by calculating the first few powers of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\).\n\nFirst, let's compute \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNext, let's compute \\(\\mathbf{A}^3\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{A}^2 \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{I},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) is the identity matrix.\n\nNow, we observe that \\(\\mathbf{A}^3 = \\mathbf{I}\\). This suggests a periodicity in the powers of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). To confirm this, let's compute \\(\\mathbf{A}^4\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^4 = \\mathbf{A}^3 \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{I} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{A}.\n\\]\n\\(\\mathbf{A}^5 = \\mathbf{A}^4 \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{A} \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix},\\)\n\\(\\mathbf{A}^6 = \\mathbf{A}^5 \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{A}^2 \\cdot \\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{I},\\)\nand so on. This confirms that \\(\\mathbf{A}^n = \\mathbf{A}\\) for \\(n \\equiv 1 \\mod 3\\), \\(\\mathbf{A}^n = \\mathbf{A}^2\\) for \\(n \\equiv 2 \\mod 3\\), and \\(\\mathbf{A}^n = \\mathbf{I}\\) for \\(n \\equiv 0 \\mod 3\\).\n\nSince \\(100 \\equiv 1 \\mod 3\\), we have \\(\\mathbf{A}^{100} = \\mathbf{A}\\). We already know that \\(\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Therefore, \\(\\mathbf{A}^{100} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
